---
import Breadcrumbs from './Breadcrumbs.astro';
import DataPipeline from './DataPipeline.astro';
import NextSteps from './NextSteps.astro';
import { dataSources } from '../lib/data-pipeline';
---

<!-- Breadcrumbs -->
<Breadcrumbs items={[
  { label: 'Home', href: '/' },
  { label: 'Core Concepts', href: '/core-concepts' },
  { label: 'Data' },
]} />

<!-- Hero -->
<header class="mb-12">
  <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 mb-4">
    <span class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-blue-violet" />
    <span class="text-xs font-mono uppercase tracking-widest text-blue-violet">Core Concepts</span>
  </span>
  <h1 class="text-2xl font-heading font-semibold text-near-black tracking-tight mb-3">
    Training Data
  </h1>
  <p class="text-base text-gray-500 leading-relaxed max-w-prose">
    A large language model is only as good as the text it reads. Before any learning begins,
    hundreds of billions of words must be gathered, cleaned, and curated into a dataset the
    model can actually learn from.
  </p>
</header>

<!-- Section: What is training data -->
<section class="mb-12">
  <h2 class="text-lg font-heading font-medium text-near-black tracking-tight mb-4">
    What is training data?
  </h2>
  <div class="prose-body text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed max-w-prose space-y-4">
    <p>
      Training data is the collection of text that a model reads during its initial learning
      phase. Think of it like the books, articles, and conversations a person studies to
      become knowledgeable — except for an AI model, this library contains hundreds of
      billions of words from across the internet.
    </p>
    <p>
      The model doesn't "read" this data the way humans do. Instead, it processes everything
      at once, looking for patterns: which words tend to appear together, how sentences are
      structured, what facts are stated, and how code is organized. The quality and diversity
      of this data directly shapes what the model can and cannot do.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- Section: Data sources -->
<section class="mb-12">
  <h2 class="text-lg font-heading font-medium text-near-black tracking-tight mb-4">
    Where does the data come from?
  </h2>
  <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-4 mb-6">
    {dataSources.map((source) => (
      <div class="rounded-lg border border-gray-200 bg-white p-5 shadow-subtle">
        <div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-2">
          <h3 class="text-sm font-medium text-near-black">{source.name}</h3>
          <span class="text-xs font-mono text-blue-violet bg-blue-violet/10 px-2 py-0.5 rounded">
            {source.share}
          </span>
        </div>
        <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">{source.description}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>
</section>

<!-- Interactive visualization -->
<section class="mb-12">
  <h2 class="text-lg font-heading font-medium text-near-black tracking-tight mb-2">
    The data curation pipeline
  </h2>
  <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed max-w-prose mb-6">
    Raw web data is noisy, repetitive, and often low-quality. Before it can train a model,
    it passes through a multi-stage pipeline that progressively filters and refines the corpus.
    Click any stage below to see what happens at that step.
  </p>
  <DataPipeline containerClass="mb-6" />
</section>

<!-- Section: Pipeline stages explained -->
<section class="mb-12">
  <h2 class="text-lg font-heading font-medium text-near-black tracking-tight mb-6">
    Pipeline stages
  </h2>
  <div class="space-y-6">
    <!-- Stage 01: Collection -->
    <div class="flex gap-4">
      <div class="flex-shrink-0 w-10 h-10 rounded-md flex items-center justify-center"
        style="color: #5E5CE6; background-color: #5E5CE612;">
        <span class="text-xs font-mono font-bold">01</span>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="text-sm font-heading font-medium text-near-black mb-1">
          Collection
        </h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
          Web crawlers systematically scan the public internet, downloading pages, documents,
          and code repositories. Common Crawl alone captures over 80 billion web pages per
          crawl cycle. Additional sources include licensed book corpora, academic preprints,
          and public code repositories.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Stage 02: Cleaning -->
    <div class="flex gap-4">
      <div class="flex-shrink-0 w-10 h-10 rounded-md flex items-center justify-center"
        style="color: #7B79D6; background-color: #7B79D612;">
        <span class="text-xs font-mono font-bold">02</span>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="text-sm font-heading font-medium text-near-black mb-1">
          Cleaning
        </h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
          Raw web pages contain navigation bars, advertisements, footers, and other
          boilerplate that repeats across thousands of pages. Cleaning strips HTML tags,
          removes repeated templates, fixes encoding errors, and discards binary or
          garbled content. This typically removes 5–15% of the raw corpus.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Stage 03: Filtering -->
    <div class="flex gap-4">
      <div class="flex-shrink-0 w-10 h-10 rounded-md flex items-center justify-center"
        style="color: #9896C6; background-color: #9896C612;">
        <span class="text-xs font-mono font-bold">03</span>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="text-sm font-heading font-medium text-near-black mb-1">
          Filtering
        </h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
          Quality classifiers score each document on readability, factual density, and
          language quality. Spam, auto-generated content, toxic text, and documents below
          a quality threshold are removed. This is the most aggressive filtering stage,
          typically discarding 20–30% of the cleaned data.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Stage 04: Deduplication -->
    <div class="flex gap-4">
      <div class="flex-shrink-0 w-10 h-10 rounded-md flex items-center justify-center"
        style="color: #B5B3B6; background-color: #B5B3B612;">
        <span class="text-xs font-mono font-bold">04</span>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="text-sm font-heading font-medium text-near-black mb-1">
          Deduplication
        </h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
          The same article often appears on dozens of websites. Deduplication identifies
          and removes near-identical documents using fuzzy hashing and similarity matching.
          This prevents the model from overlearning content that appears disproportionately
          in the dataset, keeping its training balanced.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Stage 05: Tokenization -->
    <div class="flex gap-4">
      <div class="flex-shrink-0 w-10 h-10 rounded-md flex items-center justify-center"
        style="color: #D2D0D3; background-color: #D2D0D312;">
        <span class="text-xs font-mono font-bold">05</span>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="text-sm font-heading font-medium text-near-black mb-1">
          Tokenization
        </h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
          The cleaned and filtered text is split into tokens — the smallest units the model
          processes. A tokenizer breaks "learning" into ["learn", "ing"], for example. The
          final dataset is a massive sequence of token IDs, ready for the training process.
          (See the Tokenization page for a deep dive into how this works.)
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- Section: Why quality matters -->
<section class="mb-12">
  <h2 class="text-lg font-heading font-medium text-near-black tracking-tight mb-4">
    Why data quality matters more than quantity
  </h2>
  <div class="prose-body text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed max-w-prose space-y-4">
    <p>
      Early models tried to learn from as much data as possible, regardless of quality.
      The result? Models that could recite forum spam but struggled to write coherent
      explanations. Modern research shows that a smaller, higher-quality dataset often
      produces a better model than a larger, noisier one.
    </p>
    <p>
      The key insight: models learn patterns from their training data. If the data is full
      of low-quality content, the model learns those patterns too. Careful curation ensures
      the model learns from the best examples of language, reasoning, and code.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is why top models invest heavily in data engineering — sometimes spending more
      resources on curating 200 billion tokens than on scaling to 2 trillion. The difference
      in output quality is dramatic.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<NextSteps
  accentColor="var(--color-blue-violet)"
  items={[
    { label: 'Tokenization', href: '/tokenization', description: 'How text becomes numbers' },
  ]}
/>

<style>
  .prose-body p {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }
</style>